  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nTonight and Saturday...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this evening displays a \nmid-level ridge centered over the Lower Mississippi Valley with \ntroughing moving into the Intermountain West. A shortwave \ndisturbance pushing the central/northern Plains has spun up a \nsurface low over western Nebraska earlier today. Through the day, \nthe low traversed eastward across the NE/SD border before progressing \ninto central Iowa by mid-day Saturday. The feature helped to pull a \nwarm front north into the area and allow high temperatures to climb \ninto the low to mid 90s today with dewpoints pulling into the low \n70s.\n\nStorm development occurred this evening along a surface trough \npushing into eastern Nebraska. While low-level hodographs remained \nrather impressive thanks to the LLJ, weak deep layer shear (20-25 \nkts) limited storm organization. While model soundings exhibited \n3000-3500 J/kg of MUCAPE, dry low-levels and capping limited the \namount of instability and low-level shear available to storm. With \nthis in mind, storms remained generally short-lived and prone to \nisolated pockets of strong wind gusts (40-45 mph) as storms quickly \ncollapsed and allowed for some gusty winds. \n\nA reinforcing cold front will sag southward behind the surface \ntrough tonight, helping to spark additional scattered thunderstorms \nalong and behind the front. Instability will taper off through the \novernight period, limiting additional potential for strong to severe \nstorms. Storm coverage will gradually decrease after daybreak, with \nspotty rainfall totals approaching 0.50-1.00\" under any heavier \novernight showers.\n\nA much cooler post-frontal airmass will sink into the area on \nSaturday behind the cold front, with high temperatures only expected \nto peak in the low 70s across northeast Nebraska where thick cloud \ncover remains in place. Highs are expected to increase into the mid \n80s across southeast Nebraska and far southwest Iowa, where more \nsunshine will be in place. Additional storm development is possible \nSaturday evening along the front in extreme southeast NE and \nsouthwest IA. However, model guidance continues to trend towards the \nfront being south of the NE/KS line before storms initiate, keeping \nany additional storm chances capped around 30%. \n\nSunday and Beyond...\n\nA chilly and damp start to the week is expected as another shortwave \ndisturbance pushes into the northern Plains and brings another shot \nof cooler air and additional precipitation chances to the region. \nHigh temperatures are expected to peak generally in the low 60s \nSunday through Tuesday, with periodic PoPs of 40-60%, particularly \nSunday into Monday. A break in precipitation chances is expected \nTuesday and Wednesday as transient, weak mid-level ridging slides \nacross the area between disturbances. Yet another shortwave \ndisturbances is expected to track into the area by Thursday, \nbringing PoPs of 15-30%. Highs will remain in the 60s through much \nof the work week, with a few spots reaching 70 by next weekend.",
